  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist.\n\n**Data exploration.** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` has 14,074 requests with time field `timestamp`. The fields that matter here are `response` (with `response.keyword` for exact matches), `clientip`, `url`, `geo.dest`, `bytes`, and `extension`. Response codes are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441). 503 is the only 5xx code present, so the 5xx logic is effectively a 503 count.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-4120f2de Web logs overview\"** (`eff-4120f2de-web-logs-overview-ed503445f79b`), with five panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Unique client IPs (metric)\n- Requests by response code (pie, by `response.keyword`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram)\n- 5xx responses (ES|QL metric: `WHERE response LIKE \"5*\"`)\n\nThe dashboard time range is 2026-09-01 to 2026-12-31, which covers the sample data.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-4120f2de 5xx spike\"** (`e2fbba90-09ed-4196-9249-4566f0364fa2`):\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Query matches `response.keyword` wildcard `5*`\n- Runs every 1m over a 5m window; fires when count is > 10\n- Enabled, with tag `EFF-4120f2de`. Status is currently `ok`.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- **Rule enabled.** I enabled it so it actually runs. It has no action connector, so it won't notify anyone yet.\n- **Time window is relative to now.** The sample timestamps run from late September to late November 2026, so the rule only sees documents from the last 5 minutes. It may never fire on this static data. That is expected for a rule over historical samples.\n- **Direct Elasticsearch access was not available.** Kibana's console proxy is disabled and port 9200 is unreachable, so I checked the counts through Kibana's internal search endpoint instead.",
